    {
      "id": "flight/controller/galec-production",
      "corpus": "flight-models",
      "model": "Vehicles.Rdd2.Controller",
      "entry_point": "Vehicles/package.mo",
      "why": "Flight controller. Its dynamic write index is now proven: the loop-entry integer ranges are solved as a fixpoint over the back edge, which closes EGT017 for the back substitution in LinearAlgebra.solve. What refuses it now is the allocation matrix, a dependent parameter bound to a call: Startup may call builtins only (EG028), a function only Startup calls is unreachable from DoStep (EG027), and identity(4) reaches a Real[:, :] formal as Integer[:, :] (EG017). Pinned as a refusal so the day it compiles is a reviewed diff. The elimination in the emitted LinearAlgebra.solve is now correct: the scalar local `factor` was scheduled after the two array updates that consume it and its right-hand side substituted into them, so the matrix update re-read matrix[row, column] after that same element loop had zeroed it and eliminated only the pivot column, and solve returned a matrix that was not the inverse while reporting accepted = true. The projection now assigns a value before the sibling definition that overwrites what the value reads, and carries the assigned local back into its own guard, so the matrix update reads `factor` instead of re-deriving it (galec_equivalence::embedded_c_elimination_assigns_the_shared_factor_before_the_row_it_overwrites executes both solve shapes on all three legs against a hand-computed elimination). solve now inverts a matrix that needs no pivoting exactly (measured: A = [4,1,1; 1,5,1; 1,1,6] gives A*X = I to float32). A second, independent wrong-code defect remains and is not this one: the pivot exchange `matrix[{column, pivotRow}, :] := matrix[{pivotRow, column}, :]` is emitted as one element loop that re-reads the row it has already overwritten, so both rows end up holding the old pivot row (witness: `y[{1, 3}, :] := y[{3, 1}, :]` on [1,2,3; 4,5,6; 7,8,9] emits [7,8,9; 4,5,6; 7,8,9]). Where a pivot exchange fires, solve now returns accepted = false with X = 0 instead of a wrong inverse, which is fail-closed rather than correct. The three refusals above are unchanged and still own this row; the allocation matrix has never been checked numerically, and the exchange defect is not fixed, so both must be before this row becomes a success",
      "check": {
        "kind": "compile",
        "target": "galec-production"
      },
      "expect": {
        "outcome": "refused",
        "diagnostic": "EG017"
      }
    },
